hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"15935","Ya'qub narrated to us, he said, my father narrated to me, from Ibn Ishaq, he said, Salih ibn Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to me, from Mahmud ibn Labid the brother of Banu Abd al-Ashhal, from Salama ibn Salama ibn Waqsh, who was of the people of Badr, he said: We had a Jewish neighbor among Banu Abd al-Ashhal. He said: He came out to us one day from his house shortly before the mission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and stood at the gathering of Abd al-Ashhal. Salama said: And I was that day the youngest of them in age, with a burda lying in it in the courtyard of my family. So he mentioned resurrection and the Standing and the Reckoning and the Balance and Paradise and Hell, saying that to a polytheist people, owners of idols, who did not think resurrection after death would occur. So they said to him: Woe to you, O so-and-so, do you think this will happen, that people will be raised after their death to an abode with Paradise and Hell in which they are recompensed for their deeds? He said: Yes, by the One by whom oaths are sworn, one would wish to have as his share of that Fire the greatest oven in this world, heated, then made to enter it and closed upon him, and to be saved from that Fire tomorrow. They said to him: Woe to you, what is the sign of that? He said: A prophet sent from around these lands, and he pointed with his hand toward Makka and Yemen. They said: And when do you see him? He said: He looked at me, and I was the youngest of them in age, and said: If this boy exhausts his lifespan, he will live to see him. Salama said: By Allah, night and day had not passed before Allah the Exalted sent His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was alive among us. So we believed in him, and he disbelieved in him out of transgression and envy. So we said: Woe to you, O so-and-so, are you not the one who said to us concerning him what you said? He said: Yes, but it is not he."
